## Starting with the Data

I decided to examine four major UK-listed banks: **Lloyds, NatWest, Barclays and HSBC**, comparing their share price movements against the FTSE 100.

I deliberately chose banks with different business models and levels of international exposure. Lloyds and NatWest have particularly strong connections to UK retail and commercial banking, whereas HSBC operates much more internationally. I was curious whether this might affect how strongly each bank responded to changes in UK monetary policy.

My initial expectation was that the more UK-focused banks might react more strongly, although I haven't directly tested that hypothesis yet.

Using Python and the `yfinance` library, I downloaded historical share prices, trading volumes and dividend information. The analysis covered **16 scheduled Bank of England decisions across 2023 and 2024**, comprising five rate increases, nine holds and two cuts.

With four banks examined around each decision, I ended up with **64 bank-announcement observations**.

The challenge was deciding what to do with them.

## Working Out What to Measure

My first approach was to calculate the percentage change in each bank's closing share price around an announcement.

However, it became clear that simply knowing whether a share price rose or fell wasn't especially informative.

If Barclays fell by 1.5% on a particular day, that could suggest a negative reaction to the Bank of England's announcement. But what if the wider market had fallen by 2%? Barclays would actually have performed relatively well.

I therefore introduced **market-adjusted returns**, subtracting the FTSE 100's daily return from that of each bank.

This gave me a way to examine whether the banks were outperforming or underperforming a broader market benchmark.

I then expanded the analysis to include a **three-day cumulative abnormal return (CAR)**, covering the trading day before the announcement, the announcement itself and the following trading day.

The idea was to avoid drawing conclusions from just one closing price. Markets can anticipate announcements, and share prices may continue adjusting afterwards.

One example from my results illustrates why this mattered.

On **1 February 2024**, Barclays underperformed the FTSE 100 by approximately **1.49 percentage points** on announcement day. However, its three-day CAR was actually **positive at 1.04 percentage points**, largely because its relative performance improved the following day.

Two different measurement windows therefore gave quite different impressions of the same event.

Finally, I included **relative trading volume**, comparing the number of shares traded on an announcement day with the average over the preceding 20 trading days.

I wanted to examine not just how prices were changing, but whether announcements coincided with unusual levels of trading activity.

## Developing My Python Skills

Although Python wasn't new to me, this project was quite different from much of the programming I had done previously.

Through GCSE Computer Science, I had become comfortable with concepts such as variables, lists, arrays, loops and functions. However, applying those skills to historical financial data required a much better understanding of how information is structured and manipulated.

I spent a considerable amount of time learning to work with **pandas DataFrames**, which allowed me to organise data into tables with labelled rows and columns.

This built on my earlier experience with arrays, lists and structured data. Rather than working with a small dataset that I could inspect manually, I was manipulating years of observations across several different securities.

I became much more familiar with indexing, filtering and selecting particular rows, especially when dealing with dates. For example, finding an announcement in the dataset and selecting the surrounding three trading days required understanding the difference between a calendar date and a position within the trading data.

I also learnt how useful operations such as `shift()`, `rolling()` and `groupby()` could be.

`shift()` allowed me to align prices with the previous trading day's closing price when calculating returns. `rolling()` helped calculate the 20-day averages needed for relative volume, while `groupby()` allowed me to organise results according to whether the Bank of England had raised, cut or held interest rates.

These functions made it possible to carry out the same calculations consistently across all 64 observations.

Another area I developed was **visualisation using Matplotlib**. Previously, much of my programming had focused on producing a correct output. Here, I needed to think about how to present the results so that somebody else could understand them.

That meant deciding which comparisons actually justified a graph, how to label axes accurately and how to distinguish percentage returns from percentage-point differences.

I also learnt more about structuring calculated results for storage and later analysis, including exporting the final dataset to a CSV file.

It was useful to see how familiar programming concepts could be applied to a substantially different kind of problem.

## Learning the Financial Side

The financial terminology was probably just as important a learning experience as the coding.

For instance, I had heard of the FTSE 100 long before starting this project, but I hadn't properly understood how the index itself was calculated.

I learnt that it is a **free-float-adjusted market-capitalisation-weighted index**. Essentially, larger companies have a greater influence on its movements, but the calculation adjusts for shares that aren't freely available for public trading.

That matters because an index isn't simply the average percentage movement of its constituent companies. Understanding how the benchmark is constructed is important when using it to compare individual shares.

I also developed a better understanding of dividends, particularly **ex-dividend dates**.

This became relevant when I noticed that some of the announcement windows overlapped with dates on which shareholders would no longer qualify for an upcoming dividend. A share price can adjust on these dates for reasons that have little to do with monetary policy.

For example, HSBC's market-adjusted return on **9 May 2024** was approximately **−4.47 percentage points**, but that day also coincided with its ex-dividend date.

It would have been misleading to attribute that entire movement to the Bank of England.

I therefore added a check to flag dividend overlaps and calculated the results both with and without those observations.

**Five observations were flagged in total.**

This was one of the more useful additions to the project because it made me think more carefully about what my data was actually measuring, rather than treating every calculated return as equally informative.

I also became more familiar with the relationship between interest rates and bank profitability. Higher rates can benefit a bank's net interest income in some circumstances, but they can also increase borrowing costs, reduce demand for loans and raise concerns about credit quality.

The effect is therefore not necessarily as simple as higher interest rates being good for banks and lower rates being bad.

And, perhaps most importantly, markets respond to **expectations**, not just announcements. An interest rate decision that everybody anticipates may have a very different effect from one that genuinely surprises investors.

That distinction became increasingly important when interpreting my results.

## What the Results Showed

Across the full sample, I found that:

- **Rate hikes** coincided with an average three-day CAR of **−1.71 percentage points**.
- **Rate holds** coincided with an average three-day CAR of **+0.92 percentage points**.
- **Rate cuts** coincided with an average three-day CAR of **−4.06 percentage points**.

