The result for rate cuts was particularly interesting to me.

My initial intuition had been that reducing rates might support economic activity and therefore benefit bank shares. Yet the average relative performance around cuts was negative in this sample.

That doesn't necessarily contradict the economic reasoning. A cut can occur because economic conditions are weakening, and it can also affect expectations about banks' future interest income. Investors may be reacting to a combination of those factors rather than the decision itself.

There is also a major limitation: **the sample contains only two rate cuts**. The relatively large negative movements around the August 2024 announcement had a substantial influence on the average.

I also found that removing dividend-overlap observations did not reverse the direction of the average three-day CAR for hikes, holds or cuts.

That was reassuring in a limited sense, but it certainly didn't prove that the Bank of England's decisions were responsible for the movements.

The findings are descriptive, rather than evidence of a causal relationship. Other financial news, company announcements, market expectations and wider economic conditions could all have influenced the results.

I think it's important to distinguish between identifying a pattern and being able to explain it confidently.

## What I Took Away from the Project

What I enjoyed most was having to use technical and economic knowledge together.

I already knew how to write Python, but I had much less experience deciding which financial calculations were appropriate, understanding the assumptions behind them and interpreting the resulting data.

It also made me appreciate the difference between a program producing an answer and that answer actually being useful.

A calculation can be mathematically correct whilst answering a slightly different question from the one you intended. Subtracting the FTSE 100 return, for example, provides a useful benchmark comparison, but it does not completely isolate the effect of monetary policy. The banks themselves are constituents of the FTSE 100, and different banks will naturally have different sensitivities to wider market movements.

Similarly, increasing the event window from one day to three provides more information about surrounding price movements, but it also introduces more opportunities for unrelated events to influence the result.

These were considerations I hadn't appreciated properly before working with real financial data.

The project also gave me more confidence in using Python for analysis beyond the kinds of tasks I had previously encountered. Working with libraries, tabular datasets, date indexes, calculations and graphical outputs is something I would like to continue developing.

There are several directions in which I could extend the analysis. One would be to distinguish between **expected and unexpected interest rate decisions**, rather than simply categorising them as hikes, holds and cuts. Another would be to examine more directly whether UK-focused banks respond differently from internationally diversified ones.

For now, though, I'm pleased to have completed a project that has helped me develop both my programming and my understanding of financial markets.

There's plenty I would approach differently with more experience, particularly around the methodology and statistical testing, but that's part of why I wanted to do it in the first place.

The full Python code, dataset, charts and methodology are available on my [GitHub repository](https://github.com/ranveerme/Bank-of-England-Decisions-UK-Bank-Stocks).